    "text": "To determine which item Amber should buy to get the most ounces for her $7, we need to calculate the number of ounces she can get from both the candy and the chips.\n\nFirst, let's calculate the number of ounces of candy she can get:\n- The cost of each bag of candy is $1.\n- Each bag of candy contains 12 ounces.\n- Therefore, the number of bags of candy she can buy with $7 is:\n  \\[\n  \\frac{7}{1} = 7 \\text{ bags}\n  \\]\n- The total number of ounces of candy she can get is:\n  \\[\n  7 \\times 12 = 84 \\text{ ounces}\n  \\]\n\nNext, let's calculate the number of ounces of chips she can get:\n- The cost of each bag of chips is $1.40.\n- Each bag of chips contains 17 ounces.\n- Therefore, the number of bags of chips she can buy with $7 is:\n  \\[\n  \\frac{7}{1.40} = 5 \\text{ bags}\n  \\]\n- The total number of ounces of chips she can get is:\n  \\[\n  5 \\times 17 = 85 \\text{ ounces}\n  \\]\n\nComparing the two amounts, Amber can get 85 ounces of chips, which is more than the 84 ounces of candy. Therefore, the item that gives her the most ounces is the chips.\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{85}\n\\]",
